   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Reading conditions without getting lost",
     "texto": [
      "Conditions are read in three passes.\n\n**1. Obligations.** What each party has to do: «el importe restante **se abona** antes de la llegada».\n\n**2. Deadlines.** Until when: «hasta el día quince», «en el plazo de diez días», «antes de».\n\n**3. Exceptions.** They are introduced by **salvo que**, **a menos que**, **siempre que**, **excepto si**. Almost all of them take the subjunctive: «salvo que **se avise** con dos días».\n\nA trick: look for the connectors first. If you see «salvo que», you already know the exception is coming, and the exception is usually the part that is hardest."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Conditions, deadlines and exceptions",
     "texto": [
      "**Conditions** take the subjunctive: «salvo que», «a menos que», «siempre que». For example: «te lo cambio siempre que **traigas** el ticket».\n\n**With «si» there are two worlds:**\n\n- real: si + present, future → «Si **paga** el anticipo, la reserva **queda** confirmada».\n- unreal: si + imperfect, conditional → «Si **pagaras** el anticipo, la reserva **quedaría** confirmada».\n\n**Deadlines.** «Hasta» includes the day: hasta el quince means that the fifteenth still counts. «Antes de» excludes it. And «en el plazo de diez días» counts from today, not from tomorrow."
     ],
